he center of ongoing care, rather than only symptom-triggered visits. [Data: Entities (73); Relationships (75)]\\n\\nPREVENTION is treated as an ongoing activity between scheduled exam periods, complementing routine surveillance. Advanced practice providers are described as able to support prevention and examination/evaluation activities, indicating a care model where prevention counseling and routine monitoring are distributed across provider types, not limited to physicians alone. [Data: Entities (20, 3661); Relationships (88, 7509, 7511, 7510)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The network distinguishes skin cancers (from skin cells) from primary cutaneous lymphomas (from lymphocytes/B cells), reducing diagnostic category confusion\",\n      \"explanation\": \"SKIN CANCER is defined as cancers that develop from skin cells and is contrasted with PRIMARY CUTANEOUS LYMPHOMAS (PCL), which arise from abnormal lymphocytes and are explicitly stated to be distinct from skin cancer and not classified as a type of skin cancer. This distinction is a key organizing principle in the community because it changes how lesions are interpreted and what diagnostic workup is appropriate. [Data: Entities (55, 5701, 400); Relationships (12805)]\\n\\nWithin cutaneous lymphomas, CUTANEOUS B-CELL LYMPHOMA (CBCL) is characterized as originating from abnormal B cells and often being indolent (slow-growing). This positions CBCL as a specific lymphoma subtype within the broader PCL/lymphomas-of-the-skin category, rather than within the “skin cancer” umbrella. [Data: Entities (5699, 5723, 5696, 5725); Relationships (12840, 12815)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Clinical recognition is supported by lesion morphology descriptors (nodules/papule-like appearance) tied directly to CBCL presentation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"CBCL is described as commonly presenting as one or more skin nodules, and these nodules may resemble papules (small pimple-like bumps) on or just under the skin