eper invasion, emphasizing that progression risk is a distinct concern from initial lesion identification alone. [Data: Entities (809); Relationships (1181)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Treatment and care delivery workflow elements include surgical reconstruction and radiation-treatment setup practices\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Surgical reconstruction is represented through SKIN GRAFTING, which uses a SKIN GRAFT (a patch of skin transplanted to another site) to aid healing and reduce scarring. The community notes that grafting may occur the same day or a few days after a PDEMA/Mohs procedure, linking oncologic excision workflows to reconstructive steps. [Data: Entities (633, 616, 625); Relationships (860, 874)]\\n\\nRadiation therapy workflow details are also present: INK MARKS (TATTOOS) are used for POSITIONING to ensure consistent setup and alignment for DAILY TREATMENTS. This ties skin marking practices directly to technical accuracy requirements in repeated treatment delivery. [Data: Entities (4777, 4824, 4823); Relationships (10423, 10468)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The community contains an embedded testing-threshold pattern for nodules that illustrates risk-based follow-up escalation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"NODULES are described as not always cancerous and as requiring follow-up based on size and other features; a specific threshold of 10 millimeters (mm) is used to trigger additional testing when nodules meet or exceed that size. This introduces a rule-based escalation pattern consistent with risk stratification approaches in clinical testing. [Data: Entities (5414, 5424); Relationships (11999)]\\n\\nWhen nodules are below the larger-nodule threshold, the recommended next step is to repeat ultrasound and AFP in 3 to 6 months, demonstrating how surveillance intervals can be shortened in response to findings while still deferring more invasive steps. [Data: Entities (5425); Relationships (12000)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 6.